WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.350
People go to movies to be distracted and entertained.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.430
That's basically the reason movies exist.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.240
Sometimes, those movies are so good at doing
their job that they manage to distract audiences

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.250
even with glaring plot holes or lapses in
logic.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.509
Not many people kicked up a fuss over some
of the dumbest moments in these 2017 movies,

00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:19.150
so we're here to shed light on them.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.859
Get ready to tear a few holes in some of 2017's
best flicks.

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:24.560
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.619
A simple message

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.769
Spider-Man: Homecoming's ferry scene was a
lot of fun, but it never would have happened

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.160
if not for a plot hole bigger than the hole
Vulture put in that ferry.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.510
The only reason that whole sequence happened
is because Tony Stark didn't think it was

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.870
important enough to send a quick reply to
his protégé.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.550
To elaborate, let's go back to the awkward
argument between Iron Man and Spidey.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.400
"Those weapons were out there and I tried
to tell you about it but you didn't listen.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.170
None of this would have happened if you had
just listened to me."

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.300
"I did listen, kid.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.070
Who do you think called the FBI, hun?"

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.550
It's great that Stark was paying attention,
but that would have been a nice thing to mention

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.129
before Peter tried to stop everyone himself.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:06.420
Honestly, young Peter Parker wasn't acting
insubordinate when he boarded that ferry.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.520
He was simply doing his heroic duty because
he thought no one else was going to.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.680
All of the movie's troubles following the
ferry scene — like him fighting Vulture

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.640
solo in a sweatshirt instead of his high tech
suit — are a direct result of Tony's inability

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.990
to respond to messages in a timely manner.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:25.920
A simple text to say the FBI was handling
it would've saved everyone a lot of headaches.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.079
Unfortunately, the world's most notoriously
irresponsible billionaire playboy just couldn't

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:32.820
be bothered to do something that simple.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.020
Assassin incentives

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.659
Toward the final third of John Wick: Chapter
2, John breaks some rules and scores a seven-million-dollar

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:41.150
bounty on his head.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.170
Thanks to this bounty, all of the assassins
in New York City pop out of the woodwork and

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.960
try to take a stab at Wick.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.920
What doesn't make sense here is that in the
previous movie, there was a two-million-dollar

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.420
bounty on Wick's head and not one random assassin
perked up at the offer.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.770
When the offer got doubled to four-million,
only one ultra-desperate assassin tried to

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.579
take on Wick, and it didn't end well.

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:07.479
This lack of challenge stems from the fact
that Wick has a reputation for being the underworld

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:08.479
boogeyman.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.040
"John wasn't exactly the boogeyman.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.500
He was the one you sent to kill the f------
boogeyman."

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.440
Yet in John Wick 2, everyone and their grandmother
gave it a shot for seven million, boogeyman

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.460
or not.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.020
"Seven million dollars is a lot of money,
Mr. Wick."

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.580
But is it enough to warrant going after the
man who single-handedly dismantled the Russian

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.580
mob?

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.970
It just doesn't add up.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:35.970
Not-so-secret island

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.710
As great as Wonder Woman was, it was chock-full
of dumb moments.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.280
How was Steve Trevor able to infiltrate the
German army without a shred of identification

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.400
or the ability to speak German with a proper
accent?

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.450
How was Wonder Woman able to cross no-man's
land without any of these bullets hitting

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.450
her?

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.050
Moments like these were overlooked in favor
of the movie's many strong points, but it

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.420
doesn't change the fact that the plot was
sloppy.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.520
The clearest example of this is when Themyscira
is discovered by Steve Trevor, the first man

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.560
to stumble upon it — even though it's clearly
within arm's reach of England and uses an

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.610
invisibility shield that's penetrable by literally
anyone.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:18.700
Think about it: Diana and Steve use a sailboat
to reach London from Themyscira in one night,

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.290
so the Amazons' secret island has to be pretty
close - even with a tug to tow them partway.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.290
Morning.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.040
We got lucky, we caught a ride, made some
good time."

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.780
And considering Steve could randomly fly through
the island's cloaking shield, as could the

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.360
Nazi boats following him, it's ludicrous to
suggest that no one had stumbled upon Themyscira

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.180
before that.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.830
Ape ex machina

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:45.300
For such a great movie, War for the Planet
of the Apes sure stumbles when detailing how

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.250
the apes manage to outmaneuver their captors
at the film's climax: From within the confines

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:56.630
of a maximum security penitentiary that has
guards patrolling 24/7, dozens of apes are

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.330
able to sneak away via a convenient hole in
the dirt floor of their cell.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.230
Not one guard notices all the apes vanishing
from the cell.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.730
And, as if that weren't preposterous enough,
the movie then sends all the young chimps

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.200
across electrical wires, right above guards'
heads.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.200
Not one guard happens to look up?

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.430
None of those roving spotlights casts a monkey-shaped
shadow on the ground?

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:22.560
Even though all of this happens at night,
an entire internment camp's worth of chimps

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.210
aren't just going to slink away within a few
hours completely unnoticed.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.360
Forgotten blob

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.230
Toward the end of Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 2, Ego unleashes an alien tumor that

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.159
starts gobbling up Earth.

00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:40.900
This blob grows to massive proportions, wreaking
havoc on an entire town before it's stopped.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.340
Given that the prologue took place in 1980,
and the rest of the movie was set 34 years

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:51.300
later, it's pretty safe to assume all that
blobbing happened around 2014 in the MCU's

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.300
chronology.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.900
So you'd think there would be some mention
of it across the bevy of other MCU movies

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.689
that take place after that.

00:04:57.689 --> 00:05:01.569
But everyone just seems to have forgotten
about that little town in Missouri...and all

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:04.889
the places that got wrecked by Ego's blue
goo.

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:06.650
Suspicious resurrection

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:11.099
At the very end of Justice League, we see
that Superman has revived his Clark Kent alter-ego.

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:13.451
But...isn't that kind of suspicious?

00:05:13.451 --> 00:05:15.979
A little, maybe?

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:18.249
A lot of people saw him get buried.

00:05:18.249 --> 00:05:22.729
While returning from the grave might not be
a big deal for Kal-El, humans don't really

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:23.729
tend to do that.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:26.300
At least, not without something getting thrown
at them.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.260
"Now what?

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:33.960
"We'll have to get more stuff."

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.979
Even worse, Kent worked for the Daily Planet.

00:05:36.979 --> 00:05:41.830
None of those investigative journalist co-workers
thought it was fishy that his demise and return

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.949
perfectly coincided with Superman's?

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:50.010
These policemen couldn't put it together after
Lois said his name in front of everyone?

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.680
"Clark."

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.919
The only explanation is that Clark Kent was
revived solely so that he could tear his shirt

00:05:55.919 --> 00:05:57.249
open at the end of the film.

00:05:57.249 --> 00:05:59.789
That's a good reason, right?

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:01.199
Missing Sif

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:06.449
For all the fun Thor: Ragnarok provides, some
things just don't make sense — specifically,

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:08.840
the absence of the warrior Lady Sif.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.569
Basically, Sif doesn't seem to exist anymore.

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:15.969
After having a serious role in the first Thor
movie and an appearance in Thor: The Dark

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:20.599
World, Lady Sif is nowhere to be found during
the destruction of Asgard.

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:24.759
Even the Warriors Three got an ending to their
arcs, as anticlimactic as it was.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.560
The fact that Sif got snubbed just seems like
a massive oversight.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.840
Marvel head Kevin Feige did give a vague explanation
for her absence, saying:

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.720
"What has Loki been doing on that throne in
guise of Odin?

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.759
He would have to get rid of Heimdall, because
Heimdall can see everything.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:42.800
[...] which is probably an answer to a question
I've been asked a few times today: Sif was

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.169
probably banished.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:45.169
She's off somewhere."

00:06:45.169 --> 00:06:49.720
Well, that could work — but Heimdall was
banished and still got a story arc.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.669
Sif didn't even get a tacked-on death scene
or a line of dialogue that addresses her absence.

00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:57.240
She's just … not there.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.240
Silly humans

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.389
Kong: Skull Island is a big, silly movie about
a big gorilla.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:06.330
It's a lot of fun, but let's face it: You
don't go into this kind of thing expecting

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:07.789
a watertight plot.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:11.650
Still, one plot decision made absolutely no
sense.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:15.880
Here's the scoop: A team of researchers and
soldiers are tasked with infiltrating Skull

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.650
Island, which is surrounded by a permanent
storm.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:25.650
"Like the Bermuda Triangle or like that aluminum
foil hat I like to wear on the weekends."

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:29.830
To pass through this natural hazard, they
bring a massive ship … and then abandon

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.990
it in favor of a few open-doored helicopters.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:36.599
Sure, they needed the ship intact to get them
away from the island again, but they could

00:07:36.599 --> 00:07:41.330
have gotten a lot closer on the boat rather
than brave hurricane winds in a few helicopters.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.999
"How far away is this island?

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:44.840
"50 miles.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:45.840
Maybe more?

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:46.999
Take us closer."

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:48.779
"You want to launch, do it from here."

00:07:48.779 --> 00:07:51.309
Gee, thanks, random boat guy.

00:07:51.309 --> 00:07:53.270
Minimum maximum security

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:58.069
At the outset of Ghost in the Shell, super-soldier
cyborg Major is created to do the bidding

00:07:58.069 --> 00:07:59.830
of a sinister corporation.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:05.409
When she stops following orders, the company's
goons trap Major in a maximum security fortress.

00:08:05.409 --> 00:08:09.050
Well, as it turns out, the facility isn't
very secure at all.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.979
All it takes for Major to bust out of there
is a light scuffle with a few guards and then

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:14.099
some off-screen action.

00:08:14.099 --> 00:08:18.080
Had the movie played by its own rules, she
would've been stopped before she escaped from

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.719
her deactivation bed.

00:08:19.719 --> 00:08:23.470
This whole set piece, and the sequence within
it, is just poorly thought out.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.659
"Maybe next time you can design me better."

00:08:25.659 --> 00:08:27.629
Thanks for watching!

00:08:27.629 --> 00:08:30.099
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:30.099 --> 00:08:32.159
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

